course: dictionary
lesson: 3767
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and foil is one of them. To choose the right meaning, read the words around it. «Jayden in Atlanta wrapped his brother's whole desk in tin foil» is about a thin sheet of metal, because you wrap things in tin foil.
explain: Now look at «The police foiled the robbery.» Here foil is a verb, a doing word. The clue is robbery, which is a bad plan, so foiled must mean stopped. Try it in the sentence: "The police stopped the robbery" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Foil can also be a light, thin sword used in fencing, a sport where two people fight with swords. Words like fencer and match are the clues. Always check the words around the word first, then choose the meaning that fits.
fact: Foil can be a noun or a verb, so the sentence tells you which job it does.
fact: As a noun, foil is metal, usually aluminium, made into a very thin sheet for wrapping and cooking food.
fact: As a verb, «foiled» means stopped someone from doing a bad plan. «Foiling» and «foils» work the same way.
fact: A foil is also a light, thin sword used in the sport of fencing.
fact: Never put foil in a microwave, because it can spark.
exercise: «The police foiled the robbery.» What does foiled mean here? | Cooked in the oven | Wrapped in tin foil | Made into a thin sword | *Stopped a bad plan
exercise: «Jayden wrapped his brother's whole desk in tin foil.» What is foil in this sentence? | A type of paper for drawing | *A thin sheet of metal | To stop a bad plan | A light sword for fencing
exercise: «She lunged with her foil at the fencing match.» Which meaning fits? | A kind of cake | To stop someone's plan | A thin sheet of metal | *A light, thin sword
test: Why must foil never go into a microwave? | It turns the food silver | *It can spark | The food gets too cold | It melts the plate
test: «The villain's plan was foiled at the last second.» What does foiled mean? | Made very shiny | Shouted loudly | *Stopped before it worked | Wrapped up tightly
exam: Which meaning of foil fits «Keep the sandwich wrapped in foil so it stays fresh»? | *A thin sheet of metal for wrapping food | To stop someone's bad plan | A kind of thin paper | A light sword used in fencing
